## Hint 1

The path is a *parameter*, not something reconstructed afterwards. That single
decision is what makes the combinators in later exercises trivial: `array` will
pass `${path}[0]`, `object` will pass `${path}.name`, and nothing has to track
where it is.

## Hint 2

Subclassing `Error` needs one extra line to keep the name useful:

```ts
export class DecodeError extends Error {
  readonly path: string;
  constructor(path: string, detail: string) {
    super(`${path}: ${detail}`);
    this.name = "DecodeError";
    this.path = path;
  }
}
```

## Hint 3

`decodeInteger` should reuse `decodeNumber` rather than repeat the finiteness
check — that ordering is also what makes `decodeInteger("x", "$")` report
"expected a finite number" rather than a confusing integer message.

Left to you: whether `decodeLiteral` compares with `===` or with `Object.is`.
They differ for `-0` and `NaN`; only one of those can be a literal type.

