## ex3 — type state: `build()` that does not exist yet

Edit `ex3/solution.ts`. The builder tracks which required parts are still
**missing**, and `build` is only callable when none are:

```ts
export type Method = "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "DELETE";

export interface RequestSpec {
  readonly url: string;
  readonly method: Method;
  readonly headers: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
}

export type RequiredPart = "url" | "method";

export interface RequestBuilder<Missing extends RequiredPart> {
  readonly __missing?: Missing;      // phantom: never assigned at runtime
  url(value: string): RequestBuilder<Exclude<Missing, "url">>;
  method(value: Method): RequestBuilder<Exclude<Missing, "method">>;
  header(name: string, value: string): RequestBuilder<Missing>;
  build(this: RequestBuilder<never>): RequestSpec;
}

export function request(): RequestBuilder<RequiredPart>
```

- `request().url("/orders").method("GET").build()` →
  `{ url: "/orders", method: "GET", headers: {} }`
- `request().method("POST").url("/orders").header("x-trace", "1").build()` →
  headers `{ "x-trace": "1" }`; order of `url` and `method` does not matter
- `request().url("/orders").build()` is a **compile error**
- `request().build()` is a **compile error**
- `header` may be called any number of times, before or after the required
  parts, and does not change what is missing
- Later headers with the same name replace earlier ones
- The builder is immutable: reusing an intermediate value does not leak headers
  between branches

Include the two negative cases in your file, each preceded by
`// @ts-expect-error`, so that the file fails to compile if your type state
ever stops working.

```
uv run dojo test typescript v3tb05_builders_fluent_apis ex3
```

