## Challenge — a client whose response type comes from the chain (hard)

Edit `challenge/solution.ts`. Type state and inference together: the builder
refuses to send until it has a URL and a decoder, and the decoder decides what
`send()` resolves to.

```ts
export type Method = "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "DELETE";

export interface RequestSpec {
  readonly url: string;
  readonly method: Method;
  readonly headers: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
  readonly query: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
}

export type MissingPart = "url" | "decoder";

export interface Client<Missing extends MissingPart, Body> {
  readonly __missing?: Missing;
  url(value: string): Client<Exclude<Missing, "url">, Body>;
  method(value: Method): Client<Missing, Body>;
  header(name: string, value: string): Client<Missing, Body>;
  query<const Q extends Readonly<Record<string, string | number | boolean>>>(
    params: Q,
  ): Client<Missing, Body>;
  expect<T>(decode: (value: unknown) => T): Client<Exclude<Missing, "decoder">, T>;
  send(
    this: Client<never, Body>,
    transport: (spec: RequestSpec) => Promise<unknown>,
  ): Promise<Body>;
}

export function client(): Client<MissingPart, never>
```

With `const decodeUser = (v: unknown): User => { ... }`:

- `await client().url("/users/1").expect(decodeUser).send(transport)` resolves
  to a `User`, typed `User`, with no type argument anywhere
- The `transport` callback receives
  `{ url: "/users/1", method: "GET", headers: {}, query: {} }` — `GET` is the
  default method
- `.query({ page: 2, active: true })` produces
  `query: { page: "2", active: "true" }` — values are stringified
- `.header("x-trace", "1").header("x-trace", "2")` keeps `"2"`
- `client().url("/x").send(transport)` is a **compile error** — no decoder
- `client().expect(decodeUser).send(transport)` is a **compile error** — no URL
- `client().send(transport)` is a **compile error**
- A decoder that throws propagates: `send` rejects with the decoder's error

Include all three negative cases behind `// @ts-expect-error`.

